WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.309 --> 00:00:03.809
(slow paced bubbly music)

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:07.776
- This is tube food.

00:00:07.776 --> 00:00:09.968
We call it tube food because it is food

00:00:09.968 --> 00:00:12.051
that is packed in a tube.

00:00:14.185 --> 00:00:16.139
- [Narrator] U2 pilots in the air force

00:00:16.139 --> 00:00:18.117
are a lot like astronauts.

00:00:18.117 --> 00:00:20.907
They fly as hight as 72,000 feet,

00:00:20.907 --> 00:00:22.697
and there's no oxygen.

00:00:22.697 --> 00:00:26.037
So they wear pressurized
suits and helmets to breathe.

00:00:26.037 --> 00:00:30.037
The only way to eat is
to slurp through a straw.

00:00:33.298 --> 00:00:35.584
- What we do here is we make and design

00:00:35.584 --> 00:00:38.098
rations for the U.S. military.

00:00:38.098 --> 00:00:40.786
We are the only people
in the world who make it,

00:00:40.786 --> 00:00:42.869
and these are tube foods.

00:00:45.006 --> 00:00:47.838
We have approximately
19 different varieties

00:00:47.838 --> 00:00:49.678
that we make here.

00:00:49.678 --> 00:00:52.893
Apple pie, key lime pie, beef stew,

00:00:52.893 --> 00:00:54.851
truffle mac and cheese.

00:00:54.851 --> 00:00:58.351
(slow paced bubbly music)

00:01:00.598 --> 00:01:03.678
The products used to be very puréed.

00:01:03.678 --> 00:01:05.379
It looked a lot like baby food.

00:01:05.379 --> 00:01:07.718
In fact, many of the pilots used to think

00:01:07.718 --> 00:01:11.859
that Gerber manufactured
the tube-foods for them,

00:01:11.859 --> 00:01:14.548
but about five years ago
the pilots came up to us

00:01:14.548 --> 00:01:16.619
and they wanted something
that had texture,

00:01:16.619 --> 00:01:18.688
more like regular food.

00:01:18.688 --> 00:01:20.478
If you have a beef stew,

00:01:20.478 --> 00:01:23.438
they wanted to be able to taste the peas,

00:01:23.438 --> 00:01:26.310
and the carrots, and the meat,
and the potatoes separately,

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:29.143
not just as one mashed up product.

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:33.950
- [Narrator] So Dan and
his team of food scientists

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:36.558
in this lab set to work.

00:01:36.558 --> 00:01:38.878
- [Dan] We try to grind
things up so that they do have

00:01:38.878 --> 00:01:42.419
a particle size that's small
enough to fit through this tube,

00:01:42.419 --> 00:01:44.878
yet still gives the
texture that the person

00:01:44.878 --> 00:01:46.545
would normally have.

00:01:48.227 --> 00:01:49.862
- [Narrator] Those tube-food flavors

00:01:49.862 --> 00:01:52.680
aren't the only thing they've
developed in this lab.

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:55.449
From powdered cheese to instant coffee,

00:01:55.449 --> 00:01:57.432
they've invented a number of products

00:01:57.432 --> 00:02:00.472
that eventually made their
way into civilian life.

00:02:00.472 --> 00:02:02.722
So is tube food our future?

00:02:03.742 --> 00:02:06.022
- There hasn't been an
effective use of these products

00:02:06.022 --> 00:02:08.320
in this country outside of the U2,

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:12.352
but we can see where it
might be useful for students

00:02:12.352 --> 00:02:15.062
in a dormitory, campers,

00:02:15.062 --> 00:02:19.229
anyone who wants to have
an easy eat on the go item.

